# 4. AUTHENTICATION AND USER MANAGEMENT

Implement a complete authentication system.

### Registration
- Customers must register using their email and password.
- Each email address must belong to only one account.
- Password must contain more than eight characters.
- Require at least one uppercase letter.
- Require at least one lowercase letter.
- Require at least one special character.
- Validate passwords on both frontend and backend.
- Store passwords using secure password hashing.
- Never store plain-text passwords.

### Login
- Implement actual email and password verification.
- Generate secure authentication tokens.
- Implement logout and session invalidation using an appropriate token strategy.
- Handle invalid credentials and expired sessions.
- Protect authenticated routes.

### Password reset
- Implement a functional forgot-password workflow.
- Generate secure, expiring, single-use reset tokens.
- Send password reset links to registered email addresses.
- Never reveal whether an email exists through public error messages.

### Role-based access control

Implement three roles:

1. ADMIN
2. STAFF
3. CUSTOMER

ADMIN:
- Full system access.
- Create and manage staff.
- Manage products, plans, taxes and discounts.
- Manage customers and subscriptions.
- Manage invoices and payments.
- View all reports.

STAFF:
- Manage day-to-day customer operations.
- View and manage permitted subscriptions.
- Create quotations and invoices.
- Record payments.
- Cannot create staff accounts.
- Cannot modify restricted administrative configurations.

CUSTOMER:
- Access only their own account.
- View their own subscriptions.
- View their own invoices.
- View payment history.
- Manage permitted subscription actions.
- Cannot access other customers' information.

IMPORTANT:
Enforce permissions on the backend, not merely by hiding frontend buttons.

Only ADMIN must be able to create staff accounts.

